---
description: Secrets come from environment variables and never appear in the repository or in logs.
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

# Secrets

- Read secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys) only from
  environment variables. Cloud agents get them from the Cursor dashboard
  (Cloud Agents > Secrets), CI from GitHub Actions secrets, and people from a
  local `.env` file, which git ignores.
- `.env.example` lists each variable by name, with no value. Add the name in
  the PR that first reads the variable.
- Never commit a secret, or write one to a log, error message, test, PR,
  commit message, or issue; refer to it by its variable name. Don't print the
  environment (`env`) or trace commands that use a secret (`set -x`).
- If a secret you need is missing, stop and name the variable a person must
  set. Never hard-code a value or give the variable a default in code.
- If a secret was committed or logged, tell a person at once so they can
  revoke it: deleting the commit or log doesn't undo the leak. GitHub's push
  protection is a backstop, not the check, because it recognises only known
  token formats.
